131
00:14:43,930 --> 00:14:51,330
Is it okay to call someone “musician-nim”?

132
00:14:51,330 --> 00:15:03,410
Actually, I would just call them by their name + “-nim”.

133
00:15:03,410 --> 00:15:12,980
For example, if the name is Justin, I would call him “Justin-nim”.

134
00:15:12,980 --> 00:15:22,650
In Korean, you don’t add “-nim” to every profession.

135
00:15:22,650 --> 00:15:32,480
In Korean, there are expressions like “teacher-nim,” “professor-nim,” “lawyer-nim,” right?

136
00:15:32,480 --> 00:15:42,910
But in Korean, you don’t add “-nim” to every profession.

137
00:15:42,910 --> 00:15:48,060
Sometimes it sounds awkward.

138
00:15:48,060 --> 00:15:52,450
“Musician-nim” sounds a bit awkward.

139
00:15:52,450 --> 00:15:54,140
It’s a bit difficult...

140
00:15:54,140 --> 00:16:00,510
I’ll explain this more later.

141
00:16:00,510 --> 00:16:03,710
Korean honorifics.

142
00:16:03,710 --> 00:16:08,910
Korean honorifics are very difficult, everyone.

143
00:16:08,910 --> 00:16:28,630
I don’t know how old the musician is, but if they are about the same age, calling them by name + “-nim” is perfectly fine.

144
00:16:28,630 --> 00:16:40,140
Anyway, Justin, enjoy studying Korean and eat lots of delicious Korean food.

145
00:16:40,140 --> 00:16:47,100
I really like jajangmyeon and kimchi stew too.

146
00:16:47,100 --> 00:16:56,180
